      "question": "Why scale dot products by square-root dimension?",
      "step_id": "takeaway-1",
      "response_language": null,
      "saved_at": "2026-10-30T17:45:40.149Z",
      "seconds": 5.1,
      "answer": {
        "answerable": "yes",
        "steps": [
          {
            "id": "ask-094c2d1a-1",
            "say": "Large dot products push softmax into flat regions with extremely small gradients. So the authors scale the dot products by square root of dimension to counteract that effect.",
            "actions": [
              {
                "type": "focus",
                "target": "p4.s15"
              },
              {
                "type": "highlight",
                "targets": [
                  "p4.s15"
                ],
                "color": "yellow",
                "at_word": 1
              },
              {
                "type": "highlight",
                "targets": [
                  "p4.s16"
                ],
                "color": "yellow",
                "at_word": 15
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ask-094c2d1a-2",
            "say": "As background, independent components with mean zero and variance one give a dot product with mean zero and variance dk. So the dot product grows with dimension. The authors divide by square root of dimension to bring variance back to one. That keeps softmax with healthy gradients.",
            "actions": [
              {
                "type": "focus",
                "target": "p4.s22"
              },
              {
                "type": "board",
                "item": {
                  "kind": "formula",
                  "title": "Scaled dot-product attention",
                  "latex": "Attention(Q,K,V)=softmax(\\frac{QK^T}{\\sqrt{d_k}})V",
                  "terms": [
                    {
                      "latex": "QK^T",
