    {
      "question": "Why do multiple heads beat one head?",
      "step_id": "takeaway-1",
      "response_language": null,
      "saved_at": "2026-10-30T17:45:40.149Z",
      "seconds": 7.9,
      "answer": {
        "answerable": "yes",
        "steps": [
          {
            "id": "ask-ae4ad4a1-1",
            "say": "Multiple heads beat one head because they attend jointly to different subspaces, while a single head averages them. As background, averaging blurs distinct relations. The authors report single-head attention is 0.9 BLEU worse than the best setting.",
            "actions": [
              {
                "type": "focus",
                "target": "p5.s4"
              },
              {
                "type": "highlight",
                "targets": [
                  "p5.s4"
                ],
                "color": "yellow"
              },
              {
                "type": "focus",
                "target": "p9.s9",
                "at_word": 30
              },
              {
                "type": "highlight",
                "targets": [
                  "p9.s9"
                ],
                "color": "yellow",
                "at_word": 30
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ask-ae4ad4a1-2",
            "say": "The gain comes without extra cost. Each of eight heads works in a smaller dimension, so the total computational cost is similar to full single-head attention. The heads clearly learned to perform different tasks, for example different syntactic and semantic relations in the same layer.",
